Delabella (the sweep-hull Delaunay triangulation BRepMesh's Delabella
algorithms take a plane's base triangulation from): at its start, a point
cocircular with the points before it that lies beyond any edge of their
contour, not only beyond one of the two edges at the point added last, is
added to the contour (mitcad#1919).

The case: a rectangular plane face, its boundary four points. BRepMesh
gives Delabella the points with four corners of their enlarged box, each
coordinate scaled into -0.5 to 0.5, and Delabella sorts them by their
lifted z = x^2 + y^2, which ties for points on a circle round the origin:
the rectangle's corners are cocircular (exactly: an axis-aligned rectangle
stays one), and their z's are equal but for their rounding, so that their
order is the rounding's. While the points are cocircular, Delabella grows
their contour (head -> tail -> ... -> last -> head) and expects each next
point beyond one of the two edges at `last`; with the rectangle's third
corner opposite the fourth, the fourth lies beyond the edge head -> tail,
and 1070's guard ended the triangulation as failed ("a cocircular point
on neither side"): the plane was left without a triangulation (MitCad's
checked planes made it again by the default insertion). With 64-bit long
double (GCC and Clang on x86) the four z's come out ordered as their
exact values, and the third corner is never opposite the fourth; with 53
bits (MSVC, ARM) a z's rounding decides: of a sweep of 9,415 squares and
as many rectangles, 287 squares and 91 rectangles failed, and so did a
10 mm cube's six faces and perf_holes' five square notch walls (MitCad's
`cli.perf_holes_mesh`).

- The point is added on the edge a -> b of the contour it lies beyond
  (the orientation sign of a, the point and b equal to the contour's, as
  for the two edges at `last`), found by walking the contour; the contour
  goes on from it as from a point added on the edges at `last`
  (tail = a, head = b, f = (b, a, point), whose orientation is the
  contour's). The contour of cocircular points is convex, so the point
  lies beyond exactly one edge; when it does not, the guard ends the
  triangulation as before.
- Only the points the two edges at `last` did not take reach the walk:
  every triangulation Delabella made before is the same. Of the sweep, all
  squares and rectangles are triangulated with both long doubles, each in
  10 triangles covering the points' box.

Upstream: new.

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 1082.
